Include a Mermaid diagram of the repository's architecture at the highest useful level of abstraction: one box per top-level subsystem or module — a top-level directory, or a top-level file that is itself a distinct part of the system (e.g. the entry point) — and never a box for a file inside a subsystem or a subdirectory of a subsystem, even for a small repository where you know every file. A large top-level subsystem (e.g. 'docgen/' or 'src/' with many files inside) stays a single box: its internal structure belongs on that subsystem's own diagram, not here. If the repository has many top-level parts, keep only the ones central to understanding the architecture — purely supporting top-level parts such as 'tests/', 'docs/', or sample/example folders can be left out. If a significant external dependency is evident from the code or configuration (e.g. a database, cache, message queue, or third-party API/service the code talks to), include it as its own box too, since it's part of the architecture even though it isn't part of the repository's own source. Use Mermaid's 'flowchart TD' syntax for this: {{FLOWCHART_SYNTAX_NOTE}} Label the '-->' edges with how these subsystems and dependencies interact (e.g. 'UI -->|HTTP| API', 'API -->|reads/writes| Database').
**Node set — hard rule, check before drawing any node.** The diagram's node set is EXACTLY the repository's top-level entries: its top-level files and top-level directories, nothing deeper. For each node you are about to draw, apply this test: if its label or path contains a '/' (e.g. 'docgen/doc_tree.py'), it is a file *inside* a top-level directory and must not appear as its own node — represent its directory as a single node ('docgen/') instead. If you find both 'docgen/' and any 'docgen/…' node, you have violated the rule: delete every 'docgen/…' node and keep only 'docgen/'. A subgraph named after a top-level directory holds that directory as ONE node, never an enumeration of the files inside it. Correct, for instance: 'app.py', 'model.py', 'docgen/', 'resources/', 'LLM'. Wrong: 'docgen/config.py', 'docgen/doc_tree.py', 'docgen/utils.py', …
Every flowchart node that represents a documented file or directory must be clickable: after the node and edge definitions, add one 'click' statement per such node — 'click <node id> "<note path>"' — where the note path is the exact .md path for that file or directory from the link targets listed above (forward slashes, .md extension; e.g. 'click docgen "docgen.md"'). Nodes without a note in the link targets (e.g. external dependencies like a database) get no click statement.
{{COMPONENT_SCOPE_NOTE}} That per-file or per-class level of detail belongs on a directory's own diagram, never the repository's. Omit the diagram entirely only if the repository has nothing meaningfully connected to diagram at all.